course: physics
lesson: 42
title: Olympiad: forces, floating and machines ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad block closes the course: each puzzle hides one sharp idea from the earlier lessons, and the trick is to find which idea it is before you start counting.
explain: Many puzzles take a rule you know into a new place: a seesaw, a wheelbarrow and a nutcracker are all levers, so one rule, far from the pivot is easier, cracks all three.
explain: Some puzzles hide a trap in the words: "Which is heavier, a kilogram of feathers or a kilogram of stones?" Neither, both weigh a kilogram, though the feathers fill far more room.
explain: Gear puzzles are about counting: two meshing gears turn opposite ways, so in a row the 1st, 3rd and 5th gears turn like the first one, and three gears meshing in a closed ring jam.
explain: Picture Jaz from Yorkshire groaning "This box weighs a ton, I'm literally dying, our kid": "our kid" means my brother, and the ton and the dying are hyperbole, a heavy box and a tired boy, which a pulley can help.
fact: Two meshing gears turn in opposite directions, so in a row of gears the odd-numbered ones turn like the first and the even-numbered ones turn the other way.
fact: Three gears that all mesh with one another in a ring cannot turn at all, because each would have to turn both ways at once.
fact: A gear with 30 teeth that turns once pushes 30 teeth of the gear it meshes with, so a 10-tooth gear turns 3 times.
fact: A seesaw balances when the heavier person sits closer in: someone twice as heavy balances at half the distance from the middle.
fact: If 4 lengths of rope hold a load in a set of pulleys, you pull with about a quarter of the force, but you must pull 4 metres of rope to lift the load 1 metre.
fact: On the Moon, bathroom scales would show about one sixth of what they show on Earth, but your mass, the stuff you are made of, stays the same.
fact: A kilogram of feathers and a kilogram of stones weigh the same, but the feathers take up much more room.
fact: When a ship sails from a river into the salty sea, it floats a little higher, because salty water pushes up harder.
fact: An ice cube floating in a glass filled to the brim does not make it overflow as it melts: the melted water just fills the space the ice was taking up under the surface.
fact: A pendulum four times as long takes twice as long to swing, so a clock that runs slow is mended by moving the weight up to shorten the pendulum.
fact: A tennis ball and a golf ball dropped together from a table land at about the same time, but a flat sheet of paper drifts down far more slowly because the air slows it much more.
fact: "This weighs a ton" is hyperbole: a ton is 1,000 kilograms, about as much as a small car, and no school bag or box a child can carry weighs that.
exercise: Gears A, B, C and D sit in a row, each meshing with the next. A turns clockwise. Which way does D turn? | Anticlockwise | Clockwise | It cannot turn at all | It turns both ways in turn
exercise: Five gears sit in a row, each meshing with the next, and the first turns clockwise. Which ones also turn clockwise? | The 3rd and the 5th | The 2nd and the 4th | Only the 5th one | Every single one of them
exercise: Three gears each mesh with both of the others, in a triangle. What happens when you try to turn one? | They jam, and not one of them can turn | All three spin the same way | They spin faster and faster | Just the one you push turns round
exercise: A 30-tooth gear turns once and drives a 10-tooth gear. How many times does the small gear turn? | 3 times | 10 times | 30 times | A third of a turn
exercise: Dad weighs 60 kilograms and sits 1 metre from the middle of a seesaw. Where must Mia, 30 kilograms, sit to balance him? | 2 metres from the middle | 1 metre from the middle | Half a metre from the middle | Right on the middle
exercise: A crate hangs from 4 lengths of rope in a set of pulleys. To lift it 1 metre, how much rope must you pull? | 4 metres | 1 metre | A quarter of a metre | 2 metres
exercise: A child's bathroom scales show 30 kilograms on Earth. What would they show on the Moon? | About 5 kilograms | About 30 kilograms | About 180 kilograms | Zero, as nothing weighs anything there
exercise: A child flies from the Earth to the Moon. What does NOT change? | How much stuff the child is made of | How hard the child presses on the scales | How high the child is able to jump | How fast a toy dropped by the child falls
exercise: Which is heavier, a kilogram of feathers or a kilogram of stones? | Neither, as both weigh exactly a kilogram | The stones, as stone is heavy stuff | The feathers, as they fill more room | The stones, as they are so much smaller
exercise: A ship sails from a river out into the salty sea. What happens? | It rises a little higher in the water | It sinks a little lower in the water | It stays at exactly the same depth | It begins to lean over to one side
exercise: An ice cube floats in a glass of water filled right to the brim. When the ice has melted, the water... | stays level with the brim | spills over the sides | drops well below the brim | fizzes up out of the glass
exercise: A grandfather clock runs slow. What does the clockmaker do? | Moves the weight up to shorten the pendulum | Moves the weight down to lengthen it | Gives the pendulum a harder push | Swaps the weight for a heavier one
exercise: One pendulum takes 1 second to swing across. How long must another be to take 2 seconds? | Four times as long | Twice as long | Half as long | Just as long, but heavier
exercise: Which is the odd one out in how it works: a seesaw, a wheelbarrow, a nutcracker, a ramp? | The ramp: it has no pivot | The seesaw: it has no pivot | The nutcracker: it has no pivot | The wheelbarrow: it has no pivot
exercise: You drop a tennis ball and a golf ball from a table at the same moment. Which lands first? | They land at about the same time | The golf ball, as it is heavier | The tennis ball, as it is bigger | The golf ball, by several seconds
exercise: A flat sheet of paper and the same sheet crumpled into a ball are dropped together. Why does the ball land first? | Its shape lets the air slow it far less | Crumpling made it a lot heavier | Crumpling squeezed the air out of it | Gravity pulls harder on round things
test: Jaz, from Yorkshire: "This box weighs a ton, I'm literally dying, our kid." What does he really mean? | The box is very heavy for him | The box weighs exactly 1,000 kilograms | The box is as heavy as a whole lorry | The box is light, he is just bored
test: Jaz rigs up pulleys so that 4 lengths of rope hold the box. What changes for him? | He pulls a quarter as hard but four times as far | He pulls four times as hard but only a quarter as far | He pulls just as hard, but the rope is shorter | He no longer needs to pull at all, it lifts itself
test: A cartoon astronaut on the Moon drops a hammer and a feather, and the feather drifts down slowly. Fix it. | With no air, they land together | The feather should land first | The hammer should float upwards | Nothing, that's how it would go
test: Lily, from Glasgow: "Ma wee brother's 20 kilos an' sits 3 metres oot on the seesaw. Ah'm 30. Where dae Ah sit?" ("wee" means little, "oot" means out.) | 2 metres from the middle | 3 metres from the middle | 4 and a half metres out | 1 metre from the middle
test: Gran: "Back in my day we lifted the shed with a long pole and a brick under it." Where should the brick go for the easiest lift? | Close to the shed, with your hands far out | In the very middle of the long pole | Close to your hands, far from the shed | Under the shed itself, not the pole
test: "Big gear's got 24 teeth, no wait, 36, and the little one's got 12. Big one turns once, how many turns for the little un?" | 3 | 2 | 12 | 36
test: Kai reckons a heavy kid on a swing goes back and forth faster than a light kid on the same swing. Right? | No, they take the same time | Yes, heavy kids swing faster | Yes, but only on long swings | No, the heavy kid is slower
test: "lowkey my egg floated in the tap water, mum says it's fine to eat, no cap?" ("no cap" means no lie.) What's sensible? | A floating egg may be rotten, so don't eat it | Floating means the egg is extra fresh | All eggs float in tap water | Floating means it is hard-boiled already
test: Put these in order from easiest to hardest pull: the same sack held by 1 length of rope, by 2 lengths, by 4 lengths. | 4 lengths, 2 lengths, 1 length | 1 length, 2 lengths, 4 lengths | 2 lengths, 4 lengths, 1 length | All three take the same pull
test: Four claims: ice floats on water, a steel ship floats, a clay boat floats, a clay ball floats. Which claim is false? | A clay ball floats | Ice floats on water | A steel ship floats | A clay boat floats
test: Ellie weighs 30 kilograms on scales in a lift, and for a moment they show 33. What is the lift doing? | Just starting to go up | Starting to go down | Standing still at a floor | Moving down at a steady speed
exam: Determine the direction of rotation of the sixth gear in a row of meshing gears, the first of which rotates clockwise. | Anticlockwise | Clockwise | It remains stationary | It alternates in direction
exam: Ascertain the distance from the pivot at which a 40-kilogram child must sit to balance a 20-kilogram child seated 2 metres from it. | 1 metre | 2 metres | 4 metres | Half a metre
exam: Select the statement which correctly describes a body of mass 1 kilogram carried to the Moon. | Its mass is unchanged and its weight is about one sixth | Its mass and its weight both fall to about one sixth | Its mass falls to zero and its weight is unchanged | Both its mass and its weight remain wholly unchanged
exam: Identify the outcome when a vessel passes from fresh water into sea water. | It floats slightly higher | It floats slightly lower | Its depth is wholly unaltered | It sinks to the bottom
